# tools/al/ales_segment_witness.rish — the voice-activity segmenter: ALES94's classifier read across a whole clip, frame by frame, coalesced into runs (ALES95).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES95.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260814/20260814-231217_fill-ales95-lotus-voice-activity-segmenter.md
# Stands on: lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94 — the per-frame voiced/unvoiced/silent classifier), the band read through lotus/schmitt.rye's precheck (ALES92), the floor
# ceiling from lotus/meter.rye (ALES13), over lotus/timeline.rye's Clip (ALES2). ALES94 answered the oldest question in speech processing over ONE span; its own
# scope note named this rung — a real voice-activity detector adds hysteresis over TIME. A classifier reads one window; a segmenter reads a whole clip as a
# sequence of windows and reports where each kind of sound begins and ends.
#
# lotus/segment.rye splits [start, count) into consecutive frames of frame_len samples, classifies each frame through ALES94, and coalesces neighbouring frames
# of the same verdict into a run-length label sequence. The crux is that it invents no new measurement — its segmentation equals a hand-built loop of classify
# calls coalesced, so it can never drift from the classifier it sequences. The selftest proves it: a uniform clip reads as one run; a two-region clip as two
# coalesced runs whose boundary sits where the sound changes; the segments tile the span exactly (coverage) with no two adjacent runs sharing a verdict
# (coalescing); the segmentation equals the classifier sequenced across many frame lengths, floors, and splits (the crux); the source clip is byte-for-byte
# unchanged (read-only); a single frame yields one segment equal to classify; a sub-span honours its offset; a zero, ragged, or oversized frame length refuses
# BadFrame, an inverted band BadThreshold, an out-of-range span BadRange, an illegal floor BadFloor — the clip untouched. Purely local, read-only.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_segment_witness.rish

say "ales-segment: Language EN — ALES94 answered the oldest question in speech processing over ONE span (voiced, unvoiced, or silent). A classifier reads one window; a SEGMENTER reads a whole clip as a sequence of windows and reports where each kind of sound begins and ends — the hysteresis over TIME ALES94's own scope note named. Every voice-activity gate, silence trimmer, and auto-splitter in a century of audio editing is this loop: classify each frame, then coalesce neighbouring frames of the same verdict into runs."
say "ales-segment: Style Radiant — the rule is stated positively: split [start, count) into consecutive frames of frame_len samples, classify each through ALES94 (energy gate first), and coalesce. The crux stands out: it invents no new measurement — its segmentation equals a hand-built loop of classify calls coalesced, so the segmenter is the SEQUENCING rule, not a fourth reader that could disagree with the classifier it composes."
say "ales-segment: Lens TAME — one mono clip of i16 PCM READ over a span, never written; the band validated through ALES92's precheck (BadThreshold / BadRange), the floor against the peak ceiling (BadFloor), and the frame length (BadFrame — zero, oversized, or non-dividing) BEFORE any frame is read; the runs written through an out-pointer, bounded by max_segments (the clip's own ceiling); siloed to lotus/, a run-length label sequence over one clip (not a transcript, not a diarization), no adaptive floor, no smoothing hysteresis, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/segment"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/segment.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-segment: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/segment.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-segment: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-segment: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-segment' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-segment: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-segment line"
